// sentry_turret: the smallest real game domain. A fixed gun turret that replans five times a second.
//
// Teaches:
//   - A continue branch (Killzone 2): while the running plan still makes sense, a replan answers `(!continue)` and
//     the host keeps that plan. Without it a 5 Hz replan swaps targets every time the sensors rank another threat
//     first. The continue branch sits next to the behaviour it guards, inside `attack_as_turret`, not in the root.
//   - `!begin_plan` / `!end_plan` around a behaviour (Killzone 3): the host writes `(active_plan attack ?threat)`
//     when the plan starts, so the next replan can see what is running. Bookkeeping is a step of the behaviour,
//     never a priority branch of its own.
//   - Weapon choice by threat kind and range, one branch per weapon, each with its own guard (Killzone 2).
//   - Target choice in the host: the sensors publish `(threat ?t ?kind)` best-first (Space Station 14 picks the
//     target with a utility query); the domain only filters by its own rules, in that order.
//   - Every root branch has a guard that implies its task succeeds, so the plan is the same with backtracking off.
//
// Sources (re-expressed from the published description; our own text and names):
//   - Straatman, Verweij, Champandard, "Killzone 2 Multiplayer Bots", Paris Game AI Conference 2009, slides:
//     `attack_as_turret` (a continue branch, then remember the active plan and attack) and
//     `select_weapon_and_attack_as_turret` (bullets against humanoids and turrets in bullet range, rockets against
//     the rest in rocket range). https://web.archive.org/web/2015id_/http://files.aigamedev.com/coverage/GAIC09_Killzone2Bots_StraatmanChampandard.pdf
//   - Straatman, Verweij, Champandard, Morcus, Kleve, "Hierarchical AI for Multiplayer Bots in Killzone 3",
//     Game AI Pro ch. 29, listing 29.1: `!begin_plan ... !end_plan` around `select_weapon` and `!fire_weapon`.
//     https://www.gameaipro.com/GameAIPro/GameAIPro_Chapter29_Hierarchical_AI_for_Multiplayer_Bots_in_Killzone_3.pdf
//   - Space Station 14, Resources/Prototypes/NPCs/root.yml, `TurretCompound` (MIT, (c) 2017-2026 Space Wizards
//     Federation; commit 10c868011e2920efe896539c93dfe8e8e476c338): pick a target, shoot it if in range and in line
//     of sight, else spin idly. Only the shape is used, no text. https://github.com/space-wizards/space-station-14
// Licence: re-expressed (nothing copied); credits in src/examples/canon/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md.
//
// Emulations (our planner has no `!continue`, no plan comparison, no effects; see docs/htn-guidance.md "Replanning"):
//   - `(!continue)`: a plan that is only this step tells the host "keep the running plan". The paper host in
//     src/examples/canon/paper.ts does that; GuardAgent supports it with opt-in continueTask: "!continue".
//     Its H2 candidate comparison and `activePlan` check methods are separate ways to keep a plan.
//   - `(active_plan attack ?threat)`: written by the host when it runs `!begin_plan attack ?threat`, removed by
//     `!end_plan` and when the host drops the plan. Killzone 2 wrote it with `!forget` / `!remember` inside the
//     branch; Killzone 3 and Decima use the begin / end pair kept here.
//   - Killzone 3 asks the host for a line of attack during planning (`(call request_line_of_attack ...)`); here the
//     host publishes `(line_of_fire ?t)` before planning.
//
// Deviations: the continue branch also checks that its target is still attackable (Killzone 2 kept the plan on
// `(active_plan attack_as_turret)` alone and let the running action fail); the threat index Killzone 2 passes to
// `select_threat_and_attack_as_turret` is replaced by the host's best-first order; `wield` is re-entrant (nothing to
// do when the weapon is already in hand); the idle spin is one long `!sweep`.
// Not ported: Killzone's daemons and request facts, the threat selection method, weapon preference lists, the
// energy turret variant of Space Station 14 and its utility query and services, reload and overheat handling.
//
// Facts (host sensors, written before each replan):
//   (threat ?t ?kind)        visible threats, best first; kind: humanoid | turret | vehicle | drone
//   (distance_to ?t ?d)      metres
//   (line_of_fire ?t)        the gun can hit ?t from here
//   (wielded ?weapon)        bullets | rockets
//   (active_plan attack ?t)  written by the host from !begin_plan, see above
// Primitives: (!begin_plan attack ?t) (!end_plan) (!continue) (!switch_weapon ?w) (!aim_at ?t) (!fire ?w ?t)
//             (!sweep ?seconds)
// Scenarios: worlds/sentry_turret/ (npm run htn:run -- --world sentry_turret/<scenario>; --list names them); story: stories.ts.

(:domain SentryTurret top_level_domain

    (:constants SentryTurret
        (bullet_range 30.0)
        (rocket_range 80.0)
        (sweep_seconds 3.0)
    )

    (:method (behave) top_level_method

        (combat
            (and
                (#attackable ?threat)
            )
            (
                (attack_as_turret)
            )
        )

        // Single-step branch of the "one long action" kind: the plan ends, the next replan looks again.
        (scan
            ()
            (
                (!sweep @sweep_seconds)
            )
        )
    )

    // Contract: some threat is attackable. Effect: the turret fires at one.
    (:method (attack_as_turret)

        // Keep the running attack while its target is still attackable, even if the sensors now rank another
        // threat first. The next branch is more general: it is correct only because this one comes first, which
        // is what a continue branch is.
        (continue_attack
            (and
                (active_plan attack ?target)
                (#attackable ?target)
            )
            (
                (!continue)
            )
        )

        (start_attack
            (and
                (#attackable ?threat)
            )
            (
                (select_weapon_and_attack_as_turret ?threat)
            )
        )
    )

    // Contract: ?inp_threat is attackable. Effect: fired at with the weapon that suits it.
    (:method (select_weapon_and_attack_as_turret ?inp_threat)

        (use_bullets
            (and
                (#in_bullet_range ?inp_threat)
            )
            (
                (attack_with ?inp_threat bullets)
            )
        )

        (use_rockets
            (and
                (#in_rocket_range ?inp_threat)
            )
            (
                (attack_with ?inp_threat rockets)
            )
        )
    )

    // One attack, bracketed so the host knows what runs.
    (:method (attack_with ?inp_threat ?inp_weapon)

        (fire
            ()
            (
                (!begin_plan attack ?inp_threat)
                (wield ?inp_weapon)
                (!aim_at ?inp_threat)
                (!fire ?inp_weapon ?inp_threat)
                (!end_plan)
            )
        )
    )

    // Re-entrant: nothing to do when the weapon is already in hand.
    (:method (wield ?inp_weapon)

        (already_wielded
            (and
                (wielded ?inp_weapon)
            )
            ()
        )

        (switch
            ()
            (
                (!switch_weapon ?inp_weapon)
            )
        )
    )

    // A threat in line of fire that one of the weapons can reach. `?io_`: finds one when unbound, checks the given
    // one when bound (the continue branch passes its running target; an `?out_` argument must be unbound).
    (:axiom (attackable ?io_threat)
        (and
            (threat ?io_threat ?any_kind)
            (line_of_fire ?io_threat)
            (or
                (#in_bullet_range ?io_threat)
                (#in_rocket_range ?io_threat)
            )
        )
    )

    // Bullets for soft targets, rockets for the rest (Killzone 2: no rockets against humanoids and turrets).
    (:axiom (soft_target ?inp_threat)
        (or
            (threat ?inp_threat humanoid)
            (threat ?inp_threat turret)
        )
    )

    (:axiom (in_bullet_range ?inp_threat)
        (and
            (#soft_target ?inp_threat)
            (distance_to ?inp_threat ?distance)
            (<= ?distance @bullet_range)
        )
    )

    (:axiom (in_rocket_range ?inp_threat)
        (and
            (not (#soft_target ?inp_threat))
            (distance_to ?inp_threat ?distance)
            (<= ?distance @rocket_range)
        )
    )
)
